# Website publish key

This key pair authenticates to `{{ add_website_publish_user }}`, the account used by
an external site repository or CI job to publish static content for
`{{ add_website_domain }}`.

Files in this directory:

- `{{ add_website_publish_key_path }}` - private key. Copy it into the CI secret.
- `{{ add_website_publish_key_path }}.pub` - public key, authorized for the
  publish account.
- `{{ add_website_publish_readme_path }}` - this file.

The publish account has no sudo and owns only `{{ add_website_content_dir }}`.
The nginx container mounts that directory read-only.

## Key rotation

every run of the homelab `add-website` playbook replaces this key pair and revokes the
previous key. 

## Fetch the private key

As root on the gateway:

```bash
ssh root@{{ inventory_hostname }} 'cat {{ add_website_publish_key_path }}'
```

Store the output as a secret such as `WEBSITE_PUBLISH_KEY`, then write it in CI:

```bash
mkdir -p ~/.ssh
printf '%s\n' "$WEBSITE_PUBLISH_KEY" > ~/.ssh/website-publish
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/website-publish
```

## Publish content

```bash
rsync -az --delete --chmod=D755,F644 \
  -e 'ssh -i ~/.ssh/website-publish -o IdentitiesOnly=yes -o StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new' \
  dist/ {{ add_website_publish_user }}@{{ inventory_hostname }}:{{ add_website_content_dir }}/
```

`-a` skips owner and group preservation for a non-root receiver. `--chmod` keeps directories `0755` and files `0644`, which is
all nginx needs to read them.

`--delete` removes files that no longer exist in the build output, so never
place manually managed files directly in `{{ add_website_content_dir }}`.

## Verify

```bash
ssh -i ~/.ssh/website-publish {{ add_website_publish_user }}@{{ inventory_hostname }} \
  'ls -la {{ add_website_content_dir }}'
curl -I https://{{ add_website_domain }}
```

Publishing over SSH uses the existing SSH port, so no extra firewall port is
needed.
